  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 318 AM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nTuesday, the upper low over the west lifts north and east, beginning \nto flatten the ridge as more shortwave energy lifts northeast \nthrough Missouri. This will result in another slight decrease to \nafternoon highs, with most topping out in the mid 70s. Moisture is \nstill pretty meager, though the NBM has increased the spatial extent \nof ~20% PoPs in eastern Kansas early Tuesday morning (largely \noutside of the SGF CWA). Otherwise, Tuesday will be pretty similar \nwith light south/southeast winds and high clouds.\n\nWednesday, flow begins to take on more of a southwesterly component. \nThough the ridging will have begun breaking down, the WAA will allow \nhighs to warm up to near 80 once again. This continues Thursday, \nwhich looks to be the hottest day of the week with widespread \nhighs in the low 80s.\n\nTroughing will continue to try and get established with various \nshortwaves passing by, and moisture will continue to be pulled up \nfrom the Gulf. There are still timing differences at this lead time, \nbut it looks like by the end of the week we may finally have a \nsufficient combination of moisture and forcing to bring widespread \nrain to the area. At the moment, the NBM has those chances topping \nout around 50% Saturday morning, though some other global ensembles \nare indicating it could be slightly later.",
